WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.236 --> 00:00:04.740
ADBLOCK BAN:
YouTube is stupid for blocking AdBlock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:10.740
Not just from some sort of information should&nbsp;
be free and screw big corporations mindset but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:16.560
from a grounded fundamental perspective.&nbsp;
This might be confusing to hear. Wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.960
blocking AdBlock lead to more people viewing&nbsp;
ads and/or upgrading to YouTube premium? That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.060
would lead to more revenue for YouTube,&nbsp;
not less right? Well, while that argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.980
may seem true at first glance, it doesn’t&nbsp;
hold up against scrutiny and here’s why.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:39.660
EVERYONE USES IT:&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:47.400
First of all, let’s establish just how popular&nbsp;
ad block really is. As of 2021, 42.7% of internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.740
users worldwide use ad block. This number is&nbsp;
likely a good amount lower on YouTube because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:58.800
people tend to watch YouTube on mobile devices and&nbsp;
TVs where AdBlock doesn’t work but the point still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.320
stands, AdBlock is extraordinarily popular. And,&nbsp;
when you combine this with the scale of YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.300
we’re talking about hundreds of millions&nbsp;
of people. YouTube literally has 2 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.600
monthly active users, so even something that only&nbsp;
affects 10% of users adds up to 200 million people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:24.000
much less 42.7%. So, safe to say, we’re talking&nbsp;
about a lot of people which opens up YouTube to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.840
substantial reputational risk. You might be asking&nbsp;
why? After all, YouTube isn’t doing anything wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.480
by forcing ads. It’s their platform and they’re&nbsp;
providing billions of people with value whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.360
that be educational value or entertainment value.&nbsp;
So, it’s only fair that YouTube expects to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.460
some value in return whether that’s directly&nbsp;
through YouTube Premium or indirectly through ads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:54.900
That only makes sense, but the reality is that&nbsp;
what’s fair is mostly irrelevant. You see, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.820
often like to get mad at big corporations and say&nbsp;
that all these companies care about is maximizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:07.320
profit over everything else. And that’s completely&nbsp;
true, but here’s the thing, it’s the same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:13.260
with like 90% of people. The only thing that the&nbsp;
vast majority of people care about is what value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.900
they get from a business. They don’t give a crap&nbsp;
about the business making some money. They might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.120
have some sympathy towards a mom and pop shop or&nbsp;
local restaurant but with a mega corporation like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:30.240
Google, that sympathy is non existent. In fact,&nbsp;
most people are happy when these companies lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.900
money or embarrass themselves. How do I know that?&nbsp;
Well, those are usually my most viewed videos. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.660
with that being said, the average person simply&nbsp;
doesn’t care if AdBlock is losing Google money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.120
And this is extremely important to note because&nbsp;
it lays the foundation for how people will react&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.520
to an AdBlock ban. Hundreds of milllions of&nbsp;
people have been conditioned for over a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:00.060
to watch YouTube for free without any ads. When&nbsp;
you change that, they’re naturally gonna get mad.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.060
REPUTATIONAL RISK:&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.360
When hundreds of millions of users get mad, you&nbsp;
start seeing substantial backlash against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.640
company. But, this doesn’t manifest in the way&nbsp;
that you might expect. It’s not like people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:23.940
gonna throw fits on Twitter and make a scene. Wait&nbsp;
nevermind, people are already doing that but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.400
isn’t most people. Most people will never argue&nbsp;
that AdBlock is good or that YouTube is wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:36.720
for banning AdBlock because well, they know that&nbsp;
it’s mostly fair. But, what people will do is hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.060
a grudge that they express in several different&nbsp;
ways. And the perfect example of this is Netflix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:48.000
You’ve probably noticed that over the past 12&nbsp;
to 18 months, people have suddenly started to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.640
hate Netflix. If you read through YouTube comments&nbsp;
about Netflix, you’d be convinced that the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:59.820
is on the brink of collapse or something which is&nbsp;
absolutely not true. Yes, they lost subscribers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:05.880
for a quarter or two, but they’re already gaining&nbsp;
subscribers once again. Also, even if they did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:11.340
stagnate, over 200 million paying subscribers&nbsp;
is nothing to complain about. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:17.220
why are people so pessimistic about Netflix,&nbsp;
even rooting for its demise? Well, it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:22.800
just comes down to this. Netflix is increasing&nbsp;
prices, they’re implementing ads, they’re cracking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.080
down on password sharing, and they’re losing&nbsp;
content to competitors. That’s the bottomline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.320
but most people will never admit this. Instead,&nbsp;
they’ll point their finger at some BS reasons some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:39.660
of which may have even been advantages previously.&nbsp;
Take Netflix’s content catalog for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:45.660
Nowadays, it’s popular to say that there’s a lot&nbsp;
of stuff on Netflix, but none of it is good. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.580
you were sharing passwords with 4 friends and&nbsp;
only paying $4 per month, having too much content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:57.120
likely didn’t seem like a negative. In fact, I&nbsp;
bet most people viewed it as “Yo, this is awesome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.700
I’m never gonna watch most of this stuff, but the&nbsp;
fact that I can get all of this for $4 per month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:09.120
that’s insane.” But, when you have to pay full&nbsp;
price, the attitude suddenly switches to “Bro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.640
I’m paying $20 per month and they can’t even show&nbsp;
me something that I wanna watch. What is this.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:20.700
It’s the same Netflix in both scenarios. Even&nbsp;
the pricing is the same. The only difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:27.180
is password sharing but now it’s the platform&nbsp;
that’s sucks. The same sort of thing will happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:33.120
on YouTube. Most people will never blame YouTube&nbsp;
directly for blocking ad block but they’ll take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:39.060
out their anger on every other shortcoming of the&nbsp;
platform like the algorithm. It’s no secret that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.700
YouTube recommends some random stuff from time to&nbsp;
time. Normally, people take this pretty jokingly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.460
They might go onto this random videos and&nbsp;
comment “Yooo, the algorithm is drunk again”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.000
But, when they’re forced into buying&nbsp;
YouTube premium and the same thing happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.740
the attitude shifts to, “Dude, Google is&nbsp;
a trillion dollar platform and they can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:03.060
even figure out recommendations? This&nbsp;
is unacceptable.” People are naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:08.460
really good at finding justifications for their&nbsp;
grudges. In fact, that’s probably why many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:14.040
you are watching this video right now. You want&nbsp;
justification for using AdBlock. Whether this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:20.100
is fair or not is besides the point. That’s&nbsp;
simply basic human nature. And as a company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.920
it’s in YouTube’s best interest to be cognizant&nbsp;
of this bias and act accordingly. Otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.860
it may not be long until they end up with a brand&nbsp;
favorability like Netflix at a staggering 11%.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.520
PROHIBITION DOESN’T WORK:&nbsp;

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:45.000
Putting the whole branding aspect of blocking&nbsp;
adblock to the side, the reality of such bans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:52.080
is that they don’t even work. The most classic&nbsp;
example of a ban not working is prohibition. Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.140
in the 1920s, the US decided that it would be a&nbsp;
great idea to ban alcohol, but this didn’t stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:03.420
people from drinking. It simply led to illegal&nbsp;
production and black markets. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.400
it also led to a rise in gang violence and&nbsp;
organized crime. Congress would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:15.480
repeal this law 13 years later but we don’t even&nbsp;
have to look that far. We can just look at AdBlock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.880
itself. The fact that there are multiple AdBlocks&nbsp;
on the Chrome webstore itself with hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.760
millions of downloads each show that people will&nbsp;
work around rules that they don’t like. If AdBlock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:32.280
is no longer the bypass for ads, they’ll find&nbsp;
a new bypass. Maybe that means downloading an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:36.540
app that streams YouTube videos without ads&nbsp;
or downloading the videos through some sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.220
of third party application. YouTube may be&nbsp;
able to stop funny business from happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:46.980
directly on the platform but they can’t stop&nbsp;
it from happening just outside the platform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:52.980
This could easily lead to a situation in which&nbsp;
people still watch YouTube videos but just not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.980 --> 00:08:04.860
on YouTube itself. This is a massive problem for&nbsp;
YouTube because as Bill Gates said . Similarly, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:09.360
people are gonna watch YouTube videos without ads,&nbsp;
its in YouTube’s best interest that they still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.720
watch it on YouTube itself. Sure, they may not&nbsp;
make money off of these viewers, but they still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.880
have control and influence over these viewers&nbsp;
which could be monetized later down the road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.400
If people migrate over to some bootlegged version&nbsp;
of YouTube though, that’s no longer the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.880
But, it’s not just these viewers that YouTube&nbsp;
risks losing. YouTube also risks losing the casual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:39.120
viewer. The casual viewer probably isn’t gonna go&nbsp;
out of there way to pirate YouTube videos. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.100
if they’re starting to feel that watching ads is&nbsp;
annoying, they’ll likely just watch YouTube less.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:49.920
And in an era where attention is the most&nbsp;
valuable currency, YouTube is actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:56.280
trading long term attention and even addiction&nbsp;
for short term cash. Simply not a great trade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:02.160
Also, this trade may open up YouTube to&nbsp;
competition. For the longest time, YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.460
was impossible to compete against because it was&nbsp;
universally accessible and completely free. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:13.740
as Google cracks down on ads, competitors now&nbsp;
have to ability to say “Come to our platform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:21.000
we don’t have ads.” This is exactly what Netflix&nbsp;
did with cable TV. Before viewers didn’t care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:25.560
about such temptations because well they could&nbsp;
get the same experience on YouTube itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:32.880
But, as Google cracks down, such alternatives&nbsp;
may seem more and more enticing. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.740
if YouTube wants to protect their monopoly,&nbsp;
well then, allowing AdBlock is ironically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.600
their best move. If people are gonna&nbsp;
steal, you want them to steal from you.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:49.080
THE SOLUTION:&nbsp;

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:55.320
Ok all of this makes sense from a theoretical&nbsp;
standpoint. A lot of people use AdBlock so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.320 --> 00:10:00.300
clamping down so hard will no doubt piss off&nbsp;
a bunch of people and threaten the monopoly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:06.180
But then what is the alternative? How do you go&nbsp;
about monetizing viewers who don’t want to pay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:13.800
YouTube Premium and use AdBlock? Well, the answer&nbsp;
is simply what Google was already doing. Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:20.100
had it right the first time. You may not know this&nbsp;
but Google actually has their own AdBlock that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:26.760
built into Chrome and is automatically enabled.&nbsp;
This likely seems super counterintuitive for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:33.300
ad company but this is the way. For any sort of&nbsp;
sales effort, the number one priority is to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:39.780
eyeballs. Only after that can you sell or monetize&nbsp;
or whatever. Google understood this perfectly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:45.900
Their number one priority was to get people to use&nbsp;
Chrome. They didn’t want ads to be the reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:52.320
someone chose Safari or Edge over Chrome. And that&nbsp;
strategy worked out perfectly. Basically everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:58.620
started using Chrome, and once everyone was on&nbsp;
the platform, Google focused on monetization. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:04.440
with this new AdBlock crackdown on YouTube, it&nbsp;
seems like Google may have their priorities mixed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:10.500
up. Now, you may be asking, if Google never cracks&nbsp;
down, how will they ever be able to monetize?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:16.560
Well, for that, we can just look at Google search&nbsp;
once again. There’s plenty of ad blocks for Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:22.500
search ads as well. But Google didn’t ban these&nbsp;
or take them off the chrome webstore. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:28.380
they straight up just partnered with them.&nbsp;
That’s right, Google pays Adblocking services to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:35.340
whitelist Google specific ads. This is why most ad&nbsp;
blockers do not work with Google search ads. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:41.460
adblockers are literally getting paid to show&nbsp;
them but they wont just show any ad. They only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:46.680
show ads that they feel are discrete and don’t&nbsp;
take away from the user experience because their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:52.200
reputation is on the line. If the ads are too&nbsp;
annoying, people will simply blame the ad block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.340 --> 00:12:00.600
not Google and that’s the genius of this strategy.&nbsp;
For a small fee, Google is able to run their ads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:06.360
without being the bad guy, and if people do get&nbsp;
annoyed, its the adblocker that takes the heat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:12.720
I think Google should have taken the same approach&nbsp;
on YouTube. Now, you might be saying that this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:19.920
misleading and it is but that’s not the goal. The&nbsp;
goal is not to trick people. The goal is to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:26.340
the ad experience so good that it’s not actually&nbsp;
a negative. Ideally, people shouldn’t even notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:33.060
that the AdBlock isn’t working. As such, the right&nbsp;
strategy isn’t to force people to watch ads by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:38.820
banning AdBlock. The right strategy is to make the&nbsp;
ad experience so pleasant that people don’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:44.460
feel the need to use AdBlock in the first place.&nbsp;
Google understood this perfectly for the longest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:50.760
time and they still definitely do. Though with the&nbsp;
recent crackdown, it looks like they’re willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:55.380
take a gamble. They’re essentially betting that&nbsp;
people are so addicted to YouTube that they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:02.520
force ads and get away with it. And they might be&nbsp;
able to with this move specifically, but that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:10.440
a slippery slope. If they can force us to watch&nbsp;
ads, why not make it 2,3,4,5 ads? And before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:16.680
know it, YouTube will be indistinguishable from&nbsp;
cable TV. Is that where YouTube’s heading? Only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:22.020
time will tell but this is not a great first&nbsp;
step and that’s why YouTube should feel stupid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:28.500
for blocking adblock. Google is clearly losing&nbsp;
their cool but someone who isn’t is Microsoft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:33.120
If you’d like to learn about how Microsoft&nbsp;
gave up their ego, check out this next video.

